.TH FSDIFF 1 2026-10-28 "agentutils 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fsdiff \- show what changed in a fork since it was made, as a patch
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fsdiff
.RB [ \-\-stat ]
.RI [ fork ]
.br
.B fsdiff
.RB [ \-\-stat ]
.I old new
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fsdiff
shows what changed in
.IR fork ,
or in the working directory if none is given, since
.BR fsfork (1)
made it, as a patch: the difference between the fork and its base, which is
next to it and named after it,
.I .turso.1.base
for
.IR turso.1 .
What changed in the directory it was forked from since is left out.
Given two directories,
.B fsdiff
shows the difference between them instead, from
.I old
to
.IR new .
.PP
The patch is in the form
.BR git\-diff (1)
writes, with binary files, so that
.BR git\-apply (1)
applies it, to the directory or anywhere else:
.PP
.RS
.B fsdiff turso.1 | git \-C turso apply
.RE
.PP
It changes nothing itself.
With
.BR \-\-stat ,
it shows only which files changed, and how much.
.SH WHAT IS COMPARED
Everything in the two directories but:
.TP
.I .git
Git directories, anywhere.
.TP
Ignored files
Files that the
.I .gitignore
files in the base ignore, such as build output; and new files in the fork
that the fork's
.I .gitignore
files ignore.
A file that was in the base is never left out, whatever the fork's
.I .gitignore
files say.
.PP
Symbolic links are shown as links, with the path they hold, and are never
followed.
A file whose mode changed, such as one made executable, is shown so.
.PP
Only what is in the patch reaches the directory it is applied to: a change
left out of it, as to an ignored file, is not applied.
.SH SAFETY
The fork is to be read, but not trusted: the command that changed it could
have changed anything in it.
.B fsdiff
runs nothing from it.
In particular, git never uses a repository in either directory, whose
configuration could make it run any command: the two are recorded in a
repository of
.BR fsdiff 's
own, made in the temporary directory and removed after, by git with no
configuration but its defaults, and with none of the variables, such as
.BR GIT_DIR ,
that would point it at another.
The patch is then shown by git with your configuration, for colours and the
pager, but not to change what the patch says.
.SH EXIT STATUS
As
.BR diff (1):
0 if nothing changed, 1 if something did, and 2 if something went wrong,
such as a fork without a base.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show what changed in the fork you are in:
.PP
.RS
.B fsdiff
.RE
.PP
Show which files changed in
.IR turso.1 ,
then apply the changes to
.IR turso :
.PP
.RS
.nf
.B fsdiff \-\-stat turso.1
.B fsdiff turso.1 | git \-C turso apply
.fi
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
Commits made in the fork are shown as the changes they made, not as
commits.
.PP
.B git apply
in a subdirectory of a repository applies the patch's paths from the top of
the repository; give it
.B \-\-directory
to apply them under the subdirectory instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR confine (1),
.BR fsfork (1),
.BR git\-apply (1),
.BR git\-diff (1)
